# Library words before their value (notes/layers.md, sugar): `reverse xs`, `upper t`, `floor x`, a library word of one
# typed value the program leaves free (lib/prelude.warp, lib/math.warp: `floor(x: number): int from warp`) applies to
# the value after it, as `reverse(xs)` does; a synonym calls its word (`sorted xs`). A word with a default or a rest
# parameter takes no spaced argument. Rust twin: library_words.rs word_call, library_signatures.rs typed_first_arity
rewrite ($f $x) => $f($x) where $f takes one
# before several words it takes the phrase they make: `reverse sort xs` is `reverse(sort xs)`, then `reverse(sort(xs))`
rewrite ($f $x $more...) => $f(($x $more...)) where $f takes one
# A type word before its value converts it, as its call does: `int 3.7` is int(3.7), `text int 3.7` text(int(3.7))
# (braceless and braced calls take one path). Its line takes any value, `int(x): int from emitter`, so `takes one`
# (one typed value) leaves it out. Not a name nothing binds: `int x = 3` declares x; before several words it takes
# the phrase they make, `int float 3` is int(float(3))
rewrite (int $x) => int($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (float $x) => float($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (double $x) => double($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (long $x) => long($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (string $x) => string($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (text $x) => text($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (char $x) => char($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (type $x) => type($x) where $x is not unbound
rewrite (int $x $more...) => int(($x $more...))
rewrite (float $x $more...) => float(($x $more...))
rewrite (double $x $more...) => double(($x $more...))
rewrite (long $x $more...) => long(($x $more...))
rewrite (string $x $more...) => string(($x $more...))
rewrite (text $x $more...) => text(($x $more...))
rewrite (char $x $more...) => char(($x $more...))
rewrite (type $x $more...) => type(($x $more...))
